# NoiseModelling Benchmark website generator

Run from the repository root. The existing benchmark downloads NoiseModelling
versions and uses the Clisson reference input, then publishes results as a
static website. The tutorial adapter requires Python 3.11 or later and Java 17;
set `JAVA_HOME` to the selected JDK. It uses the engine's existing command-line
launcher. A controlled single-processor JVM profile can be selected explicitly:

```bash
export JAVA_HOME=/path/to/jdk-17
export JAVA_OPTS="-Xms128m -Xmx4g -XX:ActiveProcessorCount=1"
```

The heap/processor options above are optional and should be kept the same when
comparing runs. The tutorial calculation itself requests one native thread.

```bash
bash benchmark_run.sh --version v5.0.1
bash benchmark_run.sh --aggregate-only
```

Running `bash benchmark_run.sh` keeps the existing sequential Clisson workflow.
The website is generated in `website/`.

## Tutorial barrier family

The additional case uses the official NoiseModelling 5.0.1 tutorial scene
(EPSG:2154). One fixed screen footprint, building PK 1702, is evaluated at three
heights: 1, 3 and 5 metres. Each height has its own fresh database workspace.
The supported command-line workflow uses the official **v5.0.1** distribution
and exactly the pinned **5.0.1 tutorial data**. Other versions need a validated
adapter before they can run this case.

A diagnostic v5.0.0 H1 run produced the complete native export, but its stock
JVM remained alive after export. Because of this observed shutdown limitation,
the public command refuses v5.0.0. Its diagnostic script/archive pins are kept
for future adapter work; they do not establish a supported workflow.

```bash
bash benchmark_run.sh --case tutorial-barrier --version v5.0.1
bash benchmark_run.sh --aggregate-only
```

The existing engine downloader obtains the selected official distribution and
the 5.0.1 fixture distribution if needed. The command always supplies the 5.0.1
fixture directory explicitly to `input/tutorial_barrier/prepare_tutorial.py`.
The preparation script selects the engine's declared stock-script hashes,
stages the fixed tutorial resources and writes `PINS.json`. Run families
sequentially when sharing a checkout and its preparation receipt. It checks the declared resource hashes and the three stock
Groovy scripts which the case actually loads. The Groovy entry point rechecks
these bytes before importing data or loading the scripts. The tutorial assets
remain local inputs from the official distribution; they are not included in
this contribution. A separate tutorial-data redistribution grant has not been
identified; data licensing remains **UNKNOWN** here. Input and generated export
payloads stay local. Their source and licensing information is recorded in
`input/tutorial_barrier/fixture.json`.

Outputs are stored in:

```text
output/v5.0.1/tutorial-barrier/H1/
output/v5.0.1/tutorial-barrier/H3/
output/v5.0.1/tutorial-barrier/H5/
```


Each directory contains `workspace/`, `simulation.log`, `stats_v5.0.1.json`,
`RECEIVERS_LEVEL.csv`, `RECEIVERS_LEVEL.geojson`, `IDENTITY.json` and the complete
CSV observer result. Existing height directories are refused rather than
reused. Keep reference outputs in a separate checkout or directory for a
repeat comparison.

Statistics record preparation, native calculation, export and complete script
seconds. Script time ends after the full native exports and excludes the small
JSON receipts, JVM startup and shutdown. Resource staging by the preparation
script and CSV validation are separate steps outside this script timer. The separate
`website/tutorial-barrier.html` table displays all three heights; its JSON is
under `website/data/tutorial-barrier/`. Clisson results and charts keep their
existing paths. Existing CI artifacts for `output/<version>/` also include any
completed tutorial cases.

## Complete receiver comparisons

The existing `compare_versions.py` handles both numerical GeoJSON comparisons
and complete native CSV observation. Aggregation validates every available
tutorial export, compares like heights across available engine versions and
retains the existing LAEQ comparison. The CSV observer covers every column and
row, including the header and serialized order; it does not sample or
canonicalize the output.

```bash
python3 compare_versions.py --check-receivers-csv \
  output/v5.0.1/tutorial-barrier/H1/RECEIVERS_LEVEL.csv

python3 compare_versions.py --compare-receivers-csv \
  /path/to/reference/H1/RECEIVERS_LEVEL.csv \
  output/v5.0.1/tutorial-barrier/H1/RECEIVERS_LEVEL.csv
```

`IDENTITY.json` is read beside each CSV. `--identity-a` and `--identity-b` can
supply explicit sidecar paths. The identity binds the engine version, dataset
hashes, screen height and footprint, screen PK 1702, SRID 2154, configuration,
actual stock-script hashes, source counts, complete native row count and all
native column names.

Missing identities or required IDRECEIVER/PERIOD/THE_GEOM/LAEQ/LEQ columns,
duplicate receiver/period
keys, nonfinite fields, malformed rows or disagreement with the declared header/count are refused. Native
propagation can omit source receivers, so the exported row count is checked
against the native result table rather than a presumed receiver/period product.

The dataset, height, footprint, screen identifier, SRID, source-table counts and
configuration must agree between files.
Across engine versions, script hashes can differ; for repeated runs of the same
version, different loaded-script hashes are refused. Byte differences between
valid exports are reported as differences, including expected changes across
engine versions.

CSV mode emits JSON and exits with:

- `0`: a valid single export, or two valid byte-identical exports;
- `1`: valid comparable exports with different bytes;
- `2`: an invalid or incompatible export/identity contract.

Comparison and validation run after simulation, outside the recorded native
calculation and script timing.

## Fixture and observer tests

Run the existing Gradle test target with a local official 5.0.1 distribution:

```bash
./gradlew :nm_v5.0:tutorialFixtureTest \
  -PnmStockDir=/absolute/path/to/NoiseModelling_without_gui_v5.0.1 \
  -PnmJavaLanguage=17
```

This target compiles the tutorial case and runs fixture/observer unit checks;
it does not perform noise calculations. A native family run is the separate,
explicit `benchmark_run.sh --case tutorial-barrier --version v5.0.1` command.
